{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And I will give peace in the land</b> — that there will be peace among you and no man will wage war against his brother. Or: the peace is that He will cause the evil beast to cease from the land, and the sword will not pass through your land at all, but you will pursue your enemies, going out to meet them in battle, and they will flee. And by way of truth: that He will establish peace joined together in the earth, and it is the peace of all, equal to the whole." "<b>And I will cause the evil beast to cease from the land</b> — according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, who said it means He removes them from the world (Sifra, chapter 2:1), this is the plain sense: that no evil beasts will enter their land, for when there is satiety and abundance increases and the cities are full of people, beasts will not come into the settled areas. And according to the opinion of Rabbi Shimon, who said it means He causes them to cease from doing harm (ibid.), he would interpret: I will cause the evil of the beasts to cease from the land. And this is the correct view, for the land of Israel at the time of fulfillment of the commandments will be as the world was at its beginning before the sin of the first man — no beast or creeping thing will put a person to death, as they said (Berakhot 33a): it is not the snake that kills, but sin that kills.\n\nThis is what Scripture says: 'And the suckling child shall play on the hole of the asp' (Isaiah 11:8), and likewise, 'And the cow and the bear shall feed, and the lion shall eat straw like the ox' (ibid. v. 7), for predation was not among the evil beasts except because of the sin of man, for it was decreed upon him to be prey for their teeth, and predation was implanted as their nature, even to prey one upon another, as is well known — for once they prey upon man, they sometimes become yet more evil. And so Scripture says: 'And he learned to catch prey; he devoured men' (Ezekiel 19:3).\n\nNow, at the creation of the world, it is stated regarding the beasts that He gave them vegetation to eat, as it is written: 'And to every beast of the earth and to every bird of the heavens and to everything that creeps upon the earth in which there is a living soul — every green herb for food' (Genesis 1:30), and Scripture says, 'And it was so,' for that is the nature that was established in them forever. And afterward they learned predation because of the deadly sin, as I have explained. And when slaughter of living creatures was permitted to the children of Noah after the Flood, and He warned man (ibid. 9:5), 'But surely I will require your blood for your souls… the soul of man' — and not the soul of a beast at the hand of a beast of its fellow — they remained in their habit of preying. And when the land of Israel is at perfection, He will cause the evil of their habit to cease, and they will stand upon the first nature that was established in them at the time of their creation.\n\nAnd I have already mentioned some of this in the portion of the Generations of Noah (Genesis 9:6). And therefore Scripture says concerning the days of the Redeemer who comes forth from the stump of Jesse, that peace will return to the world, and predation and the evil of the beast and every creeping thing will cease, as was their nature from the beginning. And the intent there was concerning Hezekiah, whom the Holy One, blessed be He, sought to make the Messiah (Sanhedrin 94a), but their merit did not suffice for this, and the matter will come about through the Messiah who is yet to come."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:56.000542533Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}